📌 Real-life Analogy:

sleep(): Aap ne alarm lagaya aur so gaye — kisi ko bataya nahi.

wait(): Aap kehte ho: "Mujhe uthana jab kaam ho jaye" — aur aap tab tak soye rehte ho jab tak koi aapko notify na kare.


🔹 Thread.sleep(ms)

Defined in: java.lang.Thread class.

What it does:

Puts the current thread to sleep for the given time (ms).

The thread retains the lock/monitor if it holds one.

After the sleep duration, it becomes Runnable again.

Usage:

Thread.sleep(2000); // Sleep for 2 seconds


Checked Exception: Throws InterruptedException.

👉 Use when you just want to pause execution for a specific time.




🔹 Object.wait(ms)

Defined in: java.lang.Object class (not Thread).

What it does:

Used in synchronization (multithreading).

Causes the current thread to wait until:

another thread calls notify() / notifyAll() on the same object,

or the optional timeout (ms) expires.

The thread releases the monitor/lock it holds on that object.

Usage (must be in a synchronized block):

synchronized (lock) {
    lock.wait();   // Current thread waits until notified
}


Checked Exception: Throws InterruptedException.

👉 Use when you want thread communication (e.g., producer-consumer problem).

🔑 Main Differences
| Feature                    | `sleep()`                 | `wait()`                                   |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| Belongs to                 | `Thread` class            | `Object` class                             |
| Lock behavior              | Does **not** release lock | Releases lock                              |
| Usage                      | Pause execution           | Thread communication                       |
| Call inside `synchronized` | Not required              | **Required**                               |
| Wakes up by                | Time expires or interrupt | `notify()`, `notifyAll()`, or time expires |

Example (Producer-Consumer with wait() / notify())
class Shared {
    private boolean available = false;

    public synchronized void produce() throws InterruptedException {
        while (available) {
            wait(); // wait until consumed
        }
        System.out.println("Produced");
        available = true;
        notify(); // notify consumer
    }

    public synchronized void consume() throws InterruptedException {
        while (!available) {
            wait(); // wait until produced
        }
        System.out.println("Consumed");
        available = false;
        notify(); // notify producer
    }
}


✅ In short:

Use sleep() to just delay execution.

Use wait() when you need inter-thread communication with synchronization.